The VA/DoD guideline further identifies several medications as having insufficient evidence for or against use, including citalopram, escitalopram, fluoxetine, desvenlafaxine, duloxetine, and other antidepressants [N1-F1]. It also explicitly recommends against vortioxetine for PTSD [N1-F1]. This pattern shows that while SSRIs and SNRIs remain the main pharmacotherapy family, many specific agents within that family are less established than sertraline and paroxetine.

Dosage ranges and titration schedules are best described as generally low to moderate, with some exceptions for comorbidities and side effects. For sertraline, the guideline recommends 50 mg to 200 mg daily [N1-F1]. For paroxetine, the range is 20 to 60 mg daily [N1-F1]. Venlafaxine is targeted at 75 mg to 300 mg daily [N1-F1]. The VA/DoD guidance also emphasizes that dosage ranges may vary based on individual response, side effects, comorbidities, and personal preferences [N1-F1]. The evidence also notes that titration should be adjusted over time, with monitoring of response and side effects [N1-F1]. The VA/DoD clinician guide further states that benefit depends on adequate dosages and duration of treatment [N1-F1], which is important because the effect of these medications is not immediate and can take weeks to manifest.

In terms of evidence strength, the VA/DoD guideline ranks SSRIs and SNRIs as first-line pharmacotherapeutic treatment options for PTSD [N5-R1], and it specifically states that sertraline and paroxetine have the most robust empirical evidence for reducing PTSD symptoms in randomized controlled trials [N1-F1]. Venlafaxine is also strongly recommended because of large multi-site RCTs [N1-F1]. By contrast, other antidepressants have insufficient evidence for or against use [N1-F1], and vortioxetine is explicitly discouraged [N1-F1]. The non-pharmacologic context is important too: the VA/DoD guideline recommends trauma-focused psychotherapy over medication as first-line treatment when feasible, and medication is an effective option only when psychotherapy is not available or preferred [N4-F3]. That means the strongest clinical message is not just “take an antidepressant,” but “consider psychotherapy first, and antidepressants only when psychotherapy is not available or desired.”

So the concise bottom line is:
- First-line FDA-approved options: sertraline (Zoloft) and paroxetine (Paxil) [N1-R1][N1-R2][N1-F1];
- Strongly recommended but not FDA approved for PTSD: venlafaxine (Effexor) [N1-F1];
- Less-established options: citalopram, escitalopram, fluoxetine, desvenlafaxine, duloxetine, and other antidepressants [N1-F1];
- Dosage ranges: sertraline 50–200 mg, paroxetine 20–60 mg, venlafaxine 75–300 mg [N1-F1];
- Evidence strength: sertraline and paroxetine have the most robust evidence, while other antidepressants have insufficient evidence for or against use [N1-F1][N5-R1].

